# gitleaks fingerprints: `<commit>:<file>:<rule-id>:<line>`.
#
# THIS FILE IS DELIBERATELY EMPTY, and that is the finding rather than the
# absence of one.
#
# It used to hold nine fingerprints, described as "RE-VERIFIED against gitleaks
# v8.28.0". #2686's review re-ran the check and none of the nine suppressed
# anything: replacing this file with a genuinely empty one changed no scan
# result, and each named commit scanned on its own reported nothing at the named
# line. They were pinned to the wrong commit, the wrong line, or both — the
# `measurement/phase2/bin/self-test.mjs` entry named commit `e093c620…` line 97
# while the real findings sit at three other commits on lines 111 and 276. An
# ignore file full of entries that suppress nothing is worse than an empty one,
# because it reads as coverage.
#
# WHY THE SUPPRESSIONS MOVED TO `.gitleaks.toml`, AND WHY THEY BELONG THERE.
#
# A fingerprint names ONE finding in ONE commit, which is genuinely the narrowest
# suppression gitleaks offers — but it is not a durable one here. Since #2686 the
# history scan passes `--diff-merges=first-parent`, without which gitleaks sees
# no patch for a merge commit and skips a third of this repository. That flag
# also means a line is re-reported at EVERY merge commit that carried it forward,
# each with its own fingerprint, so pinning by fingerprint would require a new
# entry after every merge — an unbounded, always-stale list, on a REQUIRED check.
# Measured: one fixture password in `measurement/` is reported at three separate
# commits over the same two source lines.
#
# So every real suppression is now an EXACT-LITERAL content allowlist in
# `.gitleaks.toml`, which is pinned to the value rather than to a commit, applies
# identically to the history scan and the tree scan, and can only ever hide the
# one string it names. That file explains each entry and why the value is not a
# secret.
#
# WHEN A FINGERPRINT IS STILL THE RIGHT ANSWER: when the finding is genuinely
# tied to one historical commit and the VALUE must not be written down — a real
# credential that was committed and has since been rotated. In that case the
# rotation is the fix and the fingerprint is the bookkeeping; add it here with a
# comment naming the rotation, never the value.
#
# WHEN YOU WRITE THAT COMMENT, DESCRIBE THE VALUE — NEVER PASTE IT.
# gitleaks scans this file like any other, so a comment that reproduces the
# suppressed value in its original `NAME=value` or `key: "value"` shape becomes a
# NEW finding, in a new commit, which this file then has to suppress as well. The
# first draft of these annotations did exactly that and added four findings to a
# branch whose whole purpose was to make the scan blocking.
#
# To add one: run the scan the `Secret scan (gitleaks)` job runs, take the
# `Fingerprint` field from the JSON report, and paste it here. `SECURITY.md` →
# "Which CI security checks block a merge" has the full command.

